 Identify the selected testing framework and state whether the group has used it before.

The group has chosen pytest as the testing tool, mainly because it is simpler to set up and use than Python's built-in testing option. Most group members have not used a dedicated testing tool like this before, though a couple of members have had some light exposure to it through earlier coursework. This will largely be a new area for the group to learn together.


Explain how the team will determine that the software produces correct output rather than merely producing some output.

Running without errors is not the same as producing the right result. To check this properly, a few real policy documents will be looked up directly from the site beforehand, so there is a clear idea of what the correct output should look like. This can then be compared against what the program actually produces. The program will also be tried on messy or broken inputs, such as missing files or dead links, to make sure it responds sensibly instead of quietly giving the wrong answer. The goal is to confirm the program is getting things right, not just that it runs.


Identify evidence that would increase the team’s confidence in the software’s reliability.

Results staying consistent when tests are run more than once
Someone other than the person who wrote a section trying to find problems with it
The program still working properly on a different computer, not just the one it was built on
The scraper handling a slow or unavailable website without breaking
Old bugs not reappearing once they've already been fixed.


Resource Risk Management

The main risks to the group's progress are things like illness, clashing deadlines from other units, or a group member becoming hard to reach without warning. To manage this, tasks will be broken into smaller, more manageable pieces early on, so progress does not depend too heavily on any single person. A shared tracker will be kept so everyone can see what has been completed and what is still outstanding. Regular check-ins will also help catch problems early, rather than discovering them right before the deadline. If someone does fall behind, the smaller task structure should make it easier for others to step in and help.
